{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00146 - MONDO_1567",
  "verbalization": "Calcifediol is implicated in the development of Nephrocalcinosis through its role in inducing Hypercalcemia Disease, which is a key driver of the condition. The evidence suggests that Calcifediol is linked to multiple calcium salts—Calcium Acetate, Calcium Glucoheptonate, and Calcium Glycerophosphate—each of which is associated with Nephrocalcinosis. These calcium compounds are known to contribute to kidney stone formation and calcium deposition, which are central mechanisms underlying Nephrocalcinosis. The consistent pathway from Calcifediol to Hypercalcemia Disease, and subsequently to Nephrocalcinosis, highlights a mechanistic link where elevated calcium levels, facilitated by Calcifediol, promote the formation of calcium-based deposits in the kidneys. The overlapping indications across the three paths reinforce the notion that Calcifediol acts as a mediator in the pathogenesis of Nephrocalcinosis, likely by influencing calcium homeostasis and renal calcium excretion.",
